from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "5. Paul’s Apostleship and Its Marks"),
("p", "Claim: Paul was made an apostle by the risen Lord himself, apart from the Twelve and apart from any church, and the marks he gives of his apostleship are four: a sight of the Lord, a revelation committed to him, a commission to the Gentiles, and the work that stands as the seal of it. His commission never changes. The volume reads the passages in which he states these marks and sets them beside the qualification the Twelve met."),
("sub", "5.1 Not of men, neither by man"),
("p", "Paul opens Galatians with a denial and an affirmation: “Paul, an apostle, (not of men, neither by man, but by Jesus Christ, and God the Father, who raised him from the dead;)” (Galatians 1:1). He then says the same of his gospel: “I neither received it of man, neither was I taught it, but by the revelation of Jesus Christ” (Galatians 1:12). He names the time and the place of his separation: “when it pleased God, who separated me from my mother’s womb, and called me by his grace, to reveal his Son in me, that I might preach him among the heathen” (Galatians 1:15, 16)."),
("p", "He then tells what he did not do: “Neither went I up to Jerusalem to them which were apostles before me; but I went into Arabia, and returned again unto Damascus” (Galatians 1:17). The words “apostles before me” place the Twelve before him in time, and the verse says he did not go up to them. When he did go, after three years, it was “to see Peter,” and he “abode with him fifteen days” (Galatians 1:18). Paul’s claim is not that he outranks the Twelve. It is that his apostleship has its own source."),
("tbl", "Paul’s apostleship has its own source.", [
    ("Galatians 1:1", T("Gal 1:1"), "An apostle not of men, neither by man, but by Jesus Christ."),
    ("Galatians 1:11, 12", T("Gal 1:11-12"), "The gospel he preached was received by the revelation of Jesus Christ."),
    ("Galatians 1:15–18", T("Gal 1:15-18"), "He did not go up to the apostles before him; he went into Arabia and returned to Damascus."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "5.2 A sight of the Lord"),
("p", "To the Corinthians Paul asks, “Am I not an apostle? am I not free? have I not seen Jesus Christ our Lord? are not ye my work in the Lord?” (1 Corinthians 9:1). Two marks are named in the verse: he has seen the Lord, and the Corinthians are his work. He adds, “If I be not an apostle unto others, yet doubtless I am to you: for the seal of mine apostleship are ye in the Lord” (1 Corinthians 9:2)."),
("p", "In the list of the appearances of the risen Lord, Paul places himself last: “And last of all he was seen of me also, as of one born out of due time” (1 Corinthians 15:8). He adds, “For I am the least of the apostles, that am not meet to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God” (1 Corinthians 15:9). The sight of the Lord is the mark that links Paul to the qualification of Acts 1:22, a witness of the resurrection, and the words “out of due time” mark the difference. Peter named the qualification as having been with the Lord from John’s baptism to the ascension. Paul was not with the Lord then, and he does not claim to have been."),
("sub", "5.3 The commission"),
("p", "The Lord’s word to Ananias at Damascus states the commission: “Go thy way: for he is a chosen vessel unto me, to bear my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel” (Acts 9:15). Ananias tells Paul that “The God of our fathers hath chosen thee, that thou shouldest know his will, and see that Just One, and shouldest hear the voice of his mouth,” and that he shall be “his witness unto all men of what thou hast seen and heard” (Acts 22:14, 15). Before Agrippa Paul gives the Lord’s words to himself: “I have appeared unto thee for this purpose, to make thee a minister and a witness both of these things which thou hast seen, and of those things in the which I will appear unto thee” (Acts 26:16)."),
("p", "Paul states the commission in his letters in the same terms. He writes, “I speak to you Gentiles, inasmuch as I am the apostle of the Gentiles, I magnify mine office” (Romans 11:13). To Timothy he writes of the gospel “Whereunto I am ordained a preacher, and an apostle, (I speak the truth in Christ, and lie not;) a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and verity” (1 Timothy 2:7; compare 2 Timothy 1:11). His commission never changes: he is the apostle of the Gentiles, and his own gospel is the gospel of the grace of God. The passages that show him reasoning in the synagogues are audience-specific, and the volume does not read them as a change of commission."),
("tbl", "The commission stated three ways.", [
    ("Acts 9:15", T("Acts 9:15"), "A chosen vessel to bear the Lord’s name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel."),
    ("Acts 26:16–18", T("Acts 26:16-18"), "A minister and a witness, sent to open eyes and to turn from darkness to light."),
    ("Romans 11:13", T("Rom 11:13"), "The apostle of the Gentiles."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "5.4 The two ministries"),
("p", "Paul reports his meeting with the leaders at Jerusalem. “When they saw that the gospel of the uncircumcision was committed unto me, as the gospel of the circumcision was unto Peter” (Galatians 2:7), and “he that wrought effectually in Peter to the apostleship of the circumcision, the same was mighty in me toward the Gentiles” (Galatians 2:8). James, Cephas and John gave Paul and Barnabas “the right hands of fellowship; that we should go unto the heathen, and they unto the circumcision” (Galatians 2:9). The passage reports two ministries and two fields, each committed by the Lord, and each with a mighty working."),
("p", "The volume reports the passage as written. It does not read the two ministries as two gospels or as one gospel preached two ways, and it does not decide how they stood in the years of the overlap beyond what Galatians 2 says. It records that the apostleship of the circumcision was Peter’s and that Paul’s was toward the Gentiles, and it keeps the two apart, because the passage keeps them apart. During the overlap the circumcision apostleship ministers to Israel under the Prophecy Program, and Paul’s gospel forms the Body; the two operate concurrently and never merge, transfer or absorb one another."),
("sub", "5.5 The seal of the apostleship"),
("p", "The last mark is the work. Paul told the Corinthians, “the seal of mine apostleship are ye in the Lord” (1 Corinthians 9:2). Writing to the Romans he says what he will speak of: “For I will not dare to speak of any of those things which Christ hath not wrought by me, to make the Gentiles obedient, by word and deed, through mighty signs and wonders, by the power of the Spirit of God; so that from Jerusalem, and round about unto Illyricum, I have fully preached the gospel of Christ” (Romans 15:18, 19)."),
("p", "The verse names mighty signs and wonders as part of what Christ wrought by Paul. Chapter 6 takes the signs. Here it is enough to say that Paul counts them with his preaching and his churches as the things that stand behind his claim. They are reported in Acts as the text reports them. The volume does not assign them to Israel’s program and does not convert them into the Mystery."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Paul’s apostleship is from Jesus Christ and not from men, and it has its own source apart from the Twelve.",
    "He gives four marks: a sight of the Lord, a revelation committed to him, a commission to the Gentiles, and the work that is the seal of it.",
    "He was “born out of due time” and did not meet the qualification of Acts 1:21, 22, and he does not claim to.",
    "Galatians 2:7 to 9 reports two ministries, each committed by the Lord, and the volume keeps them apart.",
  ]),
]
